🚽 Test case: regression -- a `yap` value >= 128 must read the same way
🚽 regardless of which storage shape it's read through. A scalar `yap`
🚽 variable's own slot is always zero-extended (char_scalar_slot_value(),
🚽 ast.h): `c = 1000` stores 232, and a direct scalar read gives 232, not
🚽 -24. Packed reads (array elements, struct fields, pointer dereference)
🚽 used to read that same byte as a signed `char` -- correct storage,
🚽 wrong load. Plain `char` is signed on this platform, so bytes >= 128
🚽 came back negative from every packed path while the scalar path
🚽 stayed 0-255: confirmed, before this fix, this fixture printed
🚽 "232 -24 -24 -24", not "232 232 232 232". A `%c`/ASCII-only fixture
🚽 (ASCII tops out at 127) cannot see this at all -- every read here is
🚽 forced into integer context (`+ 0` / `%d`) specifically so the sign
🚽 disagreement, not just the byte value, is what's being checked.
gang Box {
    yap v;
};

skibidi main {
    yap c;
    c = 1000;
    yapping("%d", c + 0);

    yap a[1];
    a[0] = 1000;
    yapping("%d", a[0] + 0);

    yap *p = &a[0];
    yapping("%d", *p + 0);

    gang Box box;
    box.v = 1000;
    yapping("%d", box.v + 0);
}
